//! vm — a process's memory beyond its ELF image: the break and anonymous
//! mappings (px12, M-PX3).
//!
//! Written from man-pages `brk(2)`, `mmap(2)`, `munmap(2)`,
//! `mprotect(2)`, the uapi `asm-generic/mman-common.h` and
//! `linux/mman.h` (the PROT_* and MAP_* numbers), and black-box strace
//! of glibc-static programs on Linux (what they ask for and what Linux
//! answers). No Linux source; no other kernel's source.
//!
//! **The words** live in the process's area (kernel/sched's
//! `proc_area`, zero for a fresh process): the break's start and its
//! current value, and the mmap cursor.
//!
//! **The break** starts at the page above the highest byte of the ELF
//! image (`start`; Linux places a static program's there too, plus a
//! random offset PAX does not model). `brk(addr)` answers the current
//! break when addr is 0, below the start, or past BRK_MAX (64 MiB) above
//! it, or when a page it would need is already mapped (as Linux answers
//! a refused `brk` with the old break); otherwise it maps zeroed frames
//! (read-write, no-execute, user) for every page up to `addr`'s, or
//! unmaps and frees those above, and answers `addr`.
//!
//! **mmap** maps anonymous private memory only: a fresh zeroed frame per
//! page, allocated at the call (no demand paging), with the leaf flags
//! PROT_* asks for (PROT_WRITE adds RW, no PROT_EXEC is NX, PROT_NONE
//! takes U/S away so ring 3 cannot touch the page while its frame stays
//! counted). Without MAP_FIXED the address is chosen top-down from
//! MMAP_TOP (0x7f0000000000, below which kernel/elf loads nothing and
//! above which the stack lives), the cursor only ever moving down, so no
//! address is handed out twice in a process's life and a hint is
//! ignored; MAP_FIXED replaces whatever is mapped there, and
//! MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE answers -EEXIST instead. Refused by name, nothing
//! mapped: a mapping of a file (-ENODEV, mmap(2)'s "the underlying
//! filesystem of the specified file does not support memory mapping":
//! PAX's initramfs is read through `read`), MAP_SHARED (-EINVAL), a
//! length of 0 or a fixed address off a page (-EINVAL), an address range
//! outside the user half (-ENOMEM), no frames (-ENOMEM, what was mapped
//! given back). `munmap` frees every mapped page of a range (holes are
//! fine, as on Linux); `mprotect` rewrites the leaves of a range every
//! page of which is mapped (-ENOMEM otherwise).
//!
//! **Reservations** (px26). A mapping made with PROT_NONE takes no
//! frame: its range is kept in the area (sixteen ranges a process) and
//! its pages stay unmapped, so a touch faults, as PROT_NONE must. glibc
//! reserves this way and backs what it uses with `mprotect`: a thread's
//! stack (2 MiB and a guard page) and a thread's own heap (128 MiB, of
//! which it backs 132 KiB at first), measured on pelt's second thread.
//! `mprotect` to a protection with any access backs each reserved page
//! it covers with a zeroed frame; `munmap` and a MAP_FIXED mapping take
//! their range out of the reservations (a hole cut in the middle of one
//! needs a second entry: when none is free the tail stays reserved,
//! address space lost and nothing else). A reserved address is never
//! handed out by a later `mmap`. With every entry in use (px34: 256; sixteen before) a
//! PROT_NONE mapping is made the old way, a frame a page. A fork's
//! child inherits the reservations with the area; `execve` drops them.
//!
//! **Backed on touch** (px34: `MAP_NORESERVE`, what wolf's runtime maps
//! a task's 8 MiB stack with). A private anonymous read-write mapping
//! with MAP_NORESERVE takes no frame at the call: its range is kept as
//! a reservation of its own kind, and a page of it is backed with a
//! zeroed read-write frame the first time ring 3 touches it (`back`,
//! from kernel/user's page-fault path) or a system call's buffer covers
//! it (kernel/uaccess backs before it checks). `mprotect` to PROT_NONE of
//! part of one (the runtime's 64 KiB guard) makes that part an ordinary
//! reservation: it faults for good; to read-write it changes nothing;
//! to anything else it backs the pages now. MADV_DONTNEED and MADV_FREE
//! on a page of one give its frame back: the next touch reads zeros,
//! which both allow (`madvise(2)`). Without MAP_NORESERVE a mapping is
//! backed at the call, as before. The reservations (both kinds) are 256
//! a process (px34; 16 before), after the descriptors in the area.
//!
//! **madvise** (28, px26): MADV_DONTNEED on private anonymous memory
//! makes the pages read zero again (`madvise(2)`); PAX zeroes them in
//! place. MADV_GUARD_INSTALL (102, Linux 6.13, which a newer glibc asks
//! for below a thread's stack) is honoured, not taken as advice: the
//! pages lose user access, so a touch faults, which is all a guard is
//! for; a reserved page already faults. MADV_GUARD_REMOVE (103) gives
//! access back, read-write and no-execute: PAX keeps no protection of a
//! page beside its leaf, and a guard sits in a stack. MADV_NORMAL,
//! RANDOM, SEQUENTIAL, WILLNEED, FREE, HUGEPAGE, NOHUGEPAGE, DONTDUMP
//! and DODUMP are hints with nothing to do: 0. Any other advice is
//! refused by name, -EINVAL. A range with a page neither mapped nor
//! reserved is -ENOMEM.
//!
//! Everything is in the user half, so the process's death gives it all
//! back with its space (kernel/paging's `free_user`). A refusal's name
//! is left in the area's REFUSAL word for kernel/user to log. No region,
//! no heap.

use frames
use paging

const PAGE: u64 = 4096
const BRK_MAX: u64 = 0x4000000 // 64 MiB
const MMAP_TOP: u64 = 0x00007f0000000000
const LOW: u64 = 0x10000 // 64 KiB: Linux's default vm.mmap_min_addr

// The area's words (kernel/files and kernel/user use others).
const A_BRK_START: u64 = 0
const A_BRK: u64 = 1
const A_MMAP: u64 = 2
const A_REFUSAL: u64 = 3
const A_RESV: u64 = 4608 // px34: 256 reserved ranges, (lo, hi) each, after kernel/files' 1024 descriptors (px26: sixteen at word 222); lo 0 a free entry; hi's bit 0 set for a range backed on touch
const RESV: u64 = 256
const LAZY: u64 = 1 // in hi: the range is backed on touch (MAP_NORESERVE)

const PROT_WRITE: u64 = 0x2
const PROT_EXEC: u64 = 0x4
const PROT_KNOWN: u64 = 0x7 // READ | WRITE | EXEC
const PROT_GROWS: u64 = 0x03000000 // PROT_GROWSDOWN | PROT_GROWSUP: accepted, ignored
const PROT_BAD: u64 = 0xfcfffff8 // every other bit of the 32-bit int (-EINVAL)
const MAP_TYPE: u64 = 0x0f
const MAP_SHARED: u64 = 0x01
const MAP_PRIVATE: u64 = 0x02
const MAP_SHARED_VALIDATE: u64 = 0x03
const MAP_FIXED: u64 = 0x10
const MAP_ANONYMOUS: u64 = 0x20
const MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE: u64 = 0x100000
const MAP_NORESERVE: u64 = 0x4000 // px34
const PROT_RW: u64 = 0x3

// -errno as %rax carries it (uapi asm-generic/errno-base.h).
const NEG_ENOMEM: u64 = 0xfffffffffffffff4 // -12
const NEG_EEXIST: u64 = 0xffffffffffffffef // -17
const NEG_ENODEV: u64 = 0xffffffffffffffed // -19
const NEG_EINVAL: u64 = 0xffffffffffffffea // -22

// Refusal codes (the REFUSAL word), named by `why`.
const R_FILE: u64 = 1
const R_SHARED: u64 = 2
const R_NO_FRAMES: u64 = 3
const R_ADVICE: u64 = 4 // px26

const NEG_EAGAIN: u64 = 0xfffffffffffffff5 // -11
const PROT_NONE_BITS: u64 = 0x7
const MADV_DONTNEED: u64 = 4
const MADV_FREE: u64 = 8 // px34
const MADV_GUARD_INSTALL: u64 = 102
const MADV_GUARD_REMOVE: u64 = 103

fn get(a: u64, i: u64) -> u64 {
    // # Safety: `a` is a process area (sched.proc_area), 40960 bytes; i < 5120.
    unsafe { ((a + i * 8) as *u64).read_volatile() }
}

fn put(a: u64, i: u64, v: u64) {
    // # Safety: as `get`.
    unsafe { ((a + i * 8) as *u64).write_volatile(v) }
}

fn page_up(x: u64) -> u64 {
    (x + PAGE - 1) & !(PAGE - 1)
}

/// The refusal the last call left (0 none), cleared by reading it.
pub fn take_refusal(a: u64) -> u64 {
    let r = get(a, A_REFUSAL)
    put(a, A_REFUSAL, 0)
    r
}

/// A refusal code's name.
pub fn why(r: u64) -> str {
    if r == R_FILE {
        return "a file-backed mapping (PAX maps anonymous memory only)"
    }
    if r == R_SHARED {
        return "MAP_SHARED (PAX maps private memory only)"
    }
    if r == R_NO_FRAMES {
        return "out of frames"
    }
    if r == R_ADVICE {
        return "madvise advice PAX does not know"
    }
    ""
}

// --- reservations (px26) -------------------------------------------------------

/// Whether user address `va` lies in a reserved range (PROT_NONE, or
/// px34's backed on touch).
pub fn reserved(a: u64, va: u64) -> bool {
    reserved_from(a, va) != 0
}

fn hi_of(a: u64, i: u64) -> u64 {
    get(a, A_RESV + i * 2 + 1) & !LAZY
}

fn lazy_of(a: u64, i: u64) -> u64 {
    get(a, A_RESV + i * 2 + 1) & LAZY
}

/// Reserve [lo, hi), backed on touch when `lazy` is LAZY: false when no
/// entry is free.
fn reserve_kind(a: u64, lo: u64, hi: u64, lazy: u64) -> bool {
    var i: u64 = 0
    while i < RESV {
        if get(a, A_RESV + i * 2) == 0 {
            put(a, A_RESV + i * 2, lo)
            put(a, A_RESV + i * 2 + 1, hi | lazy)
            return true
        }
        i += 1
    }
    false
}

/// Reserve [lo, hi) (PROT_NONE): false when no entry is free.
fn reserve(a: u64, lo: u64, hi: u64) -> bool {
    reserve_kind(a, lo, hi, 0)
}

/// Take [lo, hi) out of every reservation: an entry inside it goes, one
/// that overlaps an end is cut back, one that contains it is split in
/// two (or, with no free entry, keeps its tail too). A cut keeps its
/// entry's kind.
fn unreserve(a: u64, lo: u64, hi: u64) {
    var i: u64 = 0
    while i < RESV {
        let rl = get(a, A_RESV + i * 2)
        let rh = hi_of(a, i)
        let k = lazy_of(a, i)
        if rl != 0 && lo < rh && hi > rl {
            if lo <= rl && hi >= rh {
                put(a, A_RESV + i * 2, 0)
                put(a, A_RESV + i * 2 + 1, 0)
            } else if lo <= rl {
                put(a, A_RESV + i * 2, hi)
            } else if hi >= rh {
                put(a, A_RESV + i * 2 + 1, lo | k)
            } else {
                put(a, A_RESV + i * 2 + 1, lo | k)
                let _kept = reserve_kind(a, hi, rh, k)
            }
        }
        i += 1
    }
}

/// px34: whether user page `pg` lies in a range backed on touch.
fn lazy_at(a: u64, pg: u64) -> bool {
    var i: u64 = 0
    while i < RESV {
        let lo = get(a, A_RESV + i * 2)
        if lo != 0 && pg >= lo && pg < hi_of(a, i) {
            return lazy_of(a, i) != 0
        }
        i += 1
    }
    false
}

/// px34: back user page `pg` of the space at `pml4` with a zeroed
/// read-write frame when it lies in a range backed on touch and has none
/// yet: true when it did (kernel/user's page-fault path, kernel/uaccess
/// before it checks a buffer). False otherwise, or with no frame left.
pub fn back(a: u64, pml4: u64, pg: u64) -> bool {
    if pg >= paging.USER_TOP || mapped(pml4, pg) != 0 || !lazy_at(a, pg) {
        return false
    }
    map_zero(pml4, pg, paging.RW | paging.NX)
}

/// Whether user page `pg` is taken: mapped, or reserved.
fn taken(a: u64, pml4: u64, pg: u64) -> bool {
    mapped(pml4, pg) != 0 || reserved(a, pg)
}

/// When `pg` is in a reservation: the first address past the run of
/// reserved pages it is in, else 0 (so a search can step over a whole
/// reservation, not page by page: a thread's heap reserves 128 MiB).
fn reserved_from(a: u64, pg: u64) -> u64 {
    var i: u64 = 0
    while i < RESV {
        let lo = get(a, A_RESV + i * 2)
        if lo != 0 && pg >= lo && pg < hi_of(a, i) {
            return lo
        }
        i += 1
    }
    0
}

/// The break of a process whose ELF image ends at `top` (its highest
/// PT_LOAD byte + 1): the page above it; the mmap cursor at its start
/// (px14: `execve` calls this too, on an area the old program used).
pub fn start(a: u64, top: u64) {
    put(a, A_BRK_START, page_up(top))
    put(a, A_BRK, page_up(top))
    put(a, A_MMAP, 0) // px14: execve starts the cursor afresh
    var i: u64 = 0
    while i < RESV * 2 {
        put(a, A_RESV + i, 0) // px26: and with no reservation
        i += 1
    }
}

/// The current break (0 before `start`).
pub fn current_break(a: u64) -> u64 {
    get(a, A_BRK)
}

/// The frame mapped at user page `va`, or 0.
fn mapped(pml4: u64, va: u64) -> u64 {
    paging.translate(pml4, va) else 0
}

/// Map a zeroed frame at user page `va` with `flags` (RW, NX; U/S and P
/// added). False when no frame or table is left (nothing mapped).
fn map_zero(pml4: u64, va: u64, flags: u64) -> bool {
    let pa = frames.alloc() else 0
    if pa == 0 {
        return false
    }
    let v = pa + frames.hhdm()
    var i: u64 = 0
    while i < PAGE / 8 {
        // # Safety: the frame just taken, through the HHDM.
        unsafe { ((v + i * 8) as *u64).write_volatile(0) }
        i += 1
    }
    let r = paging.map_user(pml4, va, pa, flags) else 99
    if r == 99 {
        frames.free(pa)
        return false
    }
    true
}

/// Unmap and free every mapped page of [lo, hi) (page-aligned).
fn unmap_range(pml4: u64, lo: u64, hi: u64) {
    var pg = lo
    while pg < hi {
        let pa = paging.unmap(pml4, pg) else 0
        if pa != 0 {
            frames.free(pa)
        }
        pg += PAGE
    }
}

/// `brk(addr)`: the new break, or the old one when refused.
pub fn brk(a: u64, pml4: u64, addr: u64) -> u64 {
    let lo = get(a, A_BRK_START)
    let cur = get(a, A_BRK)
    if lo == 0 || addr < lo || addr - lo > BRK_MAX {
        return cur
    }
    let have = page_up(cur)
    let want = page_up(addr)
    if want > have {
        var pg = have
        while pg < want {
            if mapped(pml4, pg) != 0 {
                return cur
            }
            pg += PAGE
        }
        pg = have
        while pg < want {
            if !map_zero(pml4, pg, paging.RW | paging.NX) {
                unmap_range(pml4, have, pg)
                return cur
            }
            pg += PAGE
        }
    } else if want < have {
        unmap_range(pml4, want, have)
    }
    put(a, A_BRK, addr)
    addr
}

/// The leaf flags for `prot` (P and U/S are map_user's): RW for
/// PROT_WRITE, NX without PROT_EXEC.
fn leaf(prot: u64) -> u64 {
    var f: u64 = 0
    if prot & PROT_WRITE != 0 {
        f = f | paging.RW
    }
    if prot & PROT_EXEC == 0 {
        f = f | paging.NX
    }
    f
}

/// The lowest address of a free run of `n` bytes (page multiple) at or
/// below the cursor; 0 when none is left above LOW.
fn pick(a: u64, pml4: u64, n: u64) -> u64 {
    var top = get(a, A_MMAP)
    if top == 0 {
        top = MMAP_TOP
    }
    while top >= LOW + n {
        let lo = top - n
        var pg = top
        var clash: u64 = 0
        while pg > lo && clash == 0 {
            pg -= PAGE
            if mapped(pml4, pg) != 0 {
                clash = pg + 1
            } else {
                let rl = reserved_from(a, pg) // px26
                if rl != 0 {
                    clash = rl + 1 // below the whole reservation
                }
            }
        }
        if clash == 0 {
            put(a, A_MMAP, lo)
            return lo
        }
        top = clash - 1 // below the mapped page
    }
    0
}

/// `mmap(addr, len, prot, flags, fd, off)`: the address, or -errno.
pub fn mmap(a: u64, pml4: u64, addr: u64, len: u64, prot: u64, flags: u64) -> u64 {
    let kind = flags & MAP_TYPE
    if kind == MAP_SHARED || kind == MAP_SHARED_VALIDATE {
        put(a, A_REFUSAL, R_SHARED)
        return NEG_EINVAL
    }
    if kind != MAP_PRIVATE || len == 0 || prot & PROT_BAD != 0 {
        return NEG_EINVAL
    }
    if flags & MAP_ANONYMOUS == 0 {
        put(a, A_REFUSAL, R_FILE)
        return NEG_ENODEV
    }
    if len > paging.USER_TOP {
        return NEG_ENOMEM
    }
    let n = page_up(len)
    var va: u64 = 0
    if flags & (MAP_FIXED | MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE) != 0 {
        if addr % PAGE != 0 {
            return NEG_EINVAL
        }
        if addr < LOW || addr >= paging.USER_TOP || n > paging.USER_TOP - addr {
            return NEG_ENOMEM
        }
        var pg = addr
        while pg < addr + n {
            if taken(a, pml4, pg) && flags & MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE != 0 && flags & MAP_FIXED == 0 {
                return NEG_EEXIST
            }
            pg += PAGE
        }
        unmap_range(pml4, addr, addr + n)
        unreserve(a, addr, addr + n) // px26
        va = addr
    } else {
        va = pick(a, pml4, n)
        if va == 0 {
            return NEG_ENOMEM
        }
    }
    if prot & PROT_KNOWN == 0 && reserve(a, va, va + n) {
        return va // px26: PROT_NONE takes no frame; `mprotect` backs what is used
    }
    if flags & MAP_NORESERVE != 0 && prot & PROT_KNOWN == PROT_RW && reserve_kind(a, va, va + n, LAZY) {
        return va // px34: backed page by page where it is touched
    }
    let fl = leaf(prot)
    var pg = va
    while pg < va + n {
        if !map_zero(pml4, pg, fl) {
            unmap_range(pml4, va, pg)
            put(a, A_REFUSAL, R_NO_FRAMES)
            return NEG_ENOMEM
        }
        if prot & PROT_KNOWN == 0 {
            let _ok = paging.protect_user(pml4, pg, fl) // PROT_NONE: no U/S
        }
        pg += PAGE
    }
    va
}

/// `munmap(addr, len)`: 0 or -EINVAL.
pub fn munmap(a: u64, pml4: u64, addr: u64, len: u64) -> u64 {
    if addr % PAGE != 0 || len == 0 || addr >= paging.USER_TOP || len > paging.USER_TOP - addr {
        return NEG_EINVAL
    }
    let n = page_up(len)
    let end = if n > paging.USER_TOP - addr { paging.USER_TOP } else { addr + n }
    unmap_range(pml4, addr, end)
    unreserve(a, addr, end) // px26
    0
}

/// `mprotect(addr, len, prot)`: 0, -EINVAL, or -ENOMEM when a page of
/// the range is neither mapped nor reserved (nothing changed then).
/// px26: a reserved page given any access is backed with a zeroed frame
/// here (-ENOMEM when the frames run out, the pages before it backed).
pub fn mprotect(a: u64, pml4: u64, addr: u64, len: u64, prot: u64) -> u64 {
    if addr % PAGE != 0 || prot & PROT_BAD != 0 {
        return NEG_EINVAL
    }
    if len == 0 {
        return 0
    }
    if addr >= paging.USER_TOP || len > paging.USER_TOP - addr {
        return NEG_ENOMEM
    }
    let end = page_up(addr + len)
    var pg = addr
    while pg < end {
        if mapped(pml4, pg) == 0 {
            let rl = reserved_from(a, pg)
            if rl == 0 {
                return NEG_ENOMEM
            }
        }
        pg += PAGE
    }
    var fl = leaf(prot)
    if prot & PROT_KNOWN != 0 {
        fl = fl | paging.US
    }
    // px34: in a range backed on touch, PROT_NONE makes the part an
    // ordinary reservation (it faults for good) and read-write leaves the
    // untouched pages to be backed when touched.
    var any_lazy = false
    pg = addr
    while pg < end && !any_lazy {
        any_lazy = mapped(pml4, pg) == 0 && lazy_at(a, pg)
        pg += PAGE
    }
    if any_lazy && prot & PROT_KNOWN == 0 {
        unreserve(a, addr, end)
        let _r = reserve(a, addr, end)
    }
    let keep_lazy = any_lazy && prot & PROT_KNOWN == PROT_RW && prot & PROT_EXEC == 0
    pg = addr
    while pg < end {
        if mapped(pml4, pg) == 0 {
            if prot & PROT_KNOWN != 0 && !(keep_lazy && lazy_at(a, pg)) {
                if !map_zero(pml4, pg, leaf(prot)) {
                    put(a, A_REFUSAL, R_NO_FRAMES)
                    return NEG_ENOMEM
                }
            }
        } else {
            let _ok = paging.protect_user(pml4, pg, fl)
        }
        pg += PAGE
    }
    0
}

/// `madvise(addr, len, advice)` (px26; the module's header says what
/// each advice does here).
pub fn madvise(a: u64, pml4: u64, addr: u64, len: u64, advice: u64) -> u64 {
    let adv = advice & 0xffffffff
    let hint = adv <= 3 || (adv >= 14 && adv <= 17)
    if !hint && adv != MADV_DONTNEED && adv != MADV_FREE && adv != MADV_GUARD_INSTALL && adv != MADV_GUARD_REMOVE {
        put(a, A_REFUSAL, R_ADVICE)
        return NEG_EINVAL
    }
    if addr % PAGE != 0 || addr >= paging.USER_TOP || len > paging.USER_TOP - addr {
        return NEG_EINVAL
    }
    if len == 0 {
        return 0
    }
    let end = page_up(addr + len)
    var pg = addr
    while pg < end {
        if mapped(pml4, pg) == 0 && reserved_from(a, pg) == 0 {
            return NEG_ENOMEM
        }
        pg += PAGE
    }
    if hint {
        return 0
    }
    pg = addr
    while pg < end {
        let pa = mapped(pml4, pg)
        if pa != 0 && (adv == MADV_DONTNEED || adv == MADV_FREE) && lazy_at(a, pg) {
            // px34: a page of a range backed on touch gives its frame
            // back; its next touch backs a zeroed one.
            let f = paging.unmap(pml4, pg) else 0
            if f != 0 {
                frames.free(f)
            }
        } else if pa != 0 && adv != MADV_FREE { // MADV_FREE elsewhere: a hint, the page keeps its frame
            if adv == MADV_DONTNEED {
                let v = (pa & !(PAGE - 1)) + frames.hhdm()
                var i: u64 = 0
                while i < PAGE / 8 {
                    // # Safety: the frame behind the caller's own page,
                    // through the HHDM.
                    unsafe { ((v + i * 8) as *u64).write_volatile(0) }
                    i += 1
                }
            } else if adv == MADV_GUARD_INSTALL {
                let _ok = paging.protect_user(pml4, pg, paging.RW | paging.NX) // no U/S: a touch faults
            } else {
                let _ok = paging.protect_user(pml4, pg, paging.RW | paging.NX | paging.US)
            }
        }
        pg += PAGE
    }
    0
}
